                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is a 9 year old Unity kitbash, built up from the Unity Ultimate FPS asset pack, although I'm hesitant to call this a full asset flip because some kind of real game was made, and I'm not sure how many of the assets are correctly made for the game and how many of them might be flipped.  That doesn't make this a great game by any length.\n\nIt's a laughably poor DooM ripoff from Russia where you wake up with a growly voice in your head and you run around shooting samey monsters.  This can obviously be a lot of fun, but not when there's almost no polish to the game, the AI is dumb as rocks, and the game just doesn't \"fit together\" right.  Here's an example, you've got a revolver as your first weapons, so it's six bullets then reload, right?  We all know that from every other shooter game in the world.  Not here!  The developer couldn't work out how to do bullet clips/wheels in UFPS, so... you just shoot and never have to reload.\n\nThat's so typical of this entire game, it's a lulzy trashheap. \n\nOn the upside, the game does feature customisable controls and resolution, so at least some of the basic, minimum requirements have been met.  Unfortunately there's a number of other technical defects and shortcomings which contribute to the game being difficult to recommend to gamers.\n\nI'm not really sure if Putrefaction 2: Void Walker is an asset flip.  Some elements of this game certainly make it seem like the developer put some effort into assembling some kind of original here.  On the other hand, a lot of the game assets don't seem like they were created by the developer, but they haven't credited any asset creators for supplying assets, which might be a case of plagiarism.  There's no evidence, so we need to give the benefit of the doubt... regardless, asset flip or not, the quality here is indistinguishable from many asset flip/cash grab scams, and that's a problem in itself.\n\nThose technical flaws drag this game well below the minimum quality standards expected of a modern PC game.\n\nPutrefaction 2: Void Walker didn't appeal much to the people who own a copy of the game, either.  It has achievements, and they show us a very clear picture that the game didn't really capture any interest from gamers.  The most commonly and easily attained achievement is \"Shotgun\", collecting the shotgun, trivial to get, but less than 13 percent of players bothered to get that far before uninstalling the game.  Hardly a success story, even the people who own this game weren't interested in it.\n\nOne of the driving elements of this shovelware game is \"achievement spam\", that is to say the game features a ridiculously large number of trivial/automatically granted achievements, 137 of them, in fact.  It's a damning indictment for this shovelware that people only bought it for easy achievements, and not because it has any serious merit as a real game.\n\nLet's talk about price and the value this represents for us gamers.  Should you buy this game?  Is it among the best the Steam catalogue has to offer?\n\nPutrefaction 2: Void Walker has the utterly unrealistic price of around $5 USD, it's not worth it given the defects and shortcomings with the product, especially considering the sheer number of completely free, much higher quality games on Steam.\n\nFor comparison, the $5 asking price for this game could get you games like \"Assetto Corsa\", \"Borderlands 2\" or \"Total War Shogun II\".  Quality, professionally made games like those are frequently on sale cheaper than this.\n\nThe quality problems and defects with this game makes the asking price totally unrealistic. It's impossible to recommend this to gamers here on Steam.\n\nAt the end of the day, my reviews are intended to try help gamers find quality and avoid the dregs (with over 140,000 games on Steam... not all of them are masterpieces).  If you think my review is wrong, definitely buy this, download it and play it... it's surely exactly the kind of game you'll love (but I doubt it will work out that way).  Game reviews are for gamers to share their thoughts about games and help gamers choose where to spend valuable time or money.   If my review helps you to grab or reject this game, then my job is done.\n",
